Review this change and record what the review finds: $ARGUMENTS

Run the review with this project's agents and record it the way `/intent` records an intent: an issue
draft I file myself, and an ADR only if I ask for one. Be fast — independent work runs in parallel —
and be trustworthy: no finding reaches the draft unless another agent has checked its evidence on its
own. The review is **read-only**: the only files you write are the issue draft (step 5) and, when I
ask, the ADR, its links and the draft's new name (step 6). Never create the GitHub issue and never
post on a pull request — I file the issue and comment myself.

Settings for this project — `/intent`'s, shared (change them with `ocgen edit intent`):
- Issue description: at most {{ max_words }} words of visible text, structured by `{{ issue_template }}`.
- ADR (an intent file): `{{ dir }}/{{ prefix }}-<number>-<slug>.md`, the number zero-padded to
  {{ digits }} digits, structured by `{{ intent_template }}`.
{% if approvers -%}
- Approvers: {{ approvers }} — they decide. GitHub notifies them through the @mentions in the issue;
  never contact them any other way.
{% else -%}
- **No approvers configured.** Say "No approvers configured" in the draft, and suggest
  `ocgen edit intent --approver @handle`.
{% endif -%}
- **The approvers can change while you work**, and this list is as of when this skill was generated.
  After every write to an issue draft or an ADR, ocgen checks it against the project's current
  approvers and tells you what is missing: its list wins — fix the file at once.
