## 0. Choose the target and pin it
Work out what to review from the arguments, then record its base and head commits (`git rev-parse`):
every agent reviews those, even if the branch moves while you work.
- **Empty:** the current branch against its base, including what isn't committed yet. The base is the
  first of: the base branch of an open pull request for this branch (`gh pr view --json baseRefName`);
  {% if branch %}`{{ branch }}`{% else %}the remote's default branch (`git ls-remote --symref origin HEAD` prints `ref: refs/heads/<branch>`){% endif %};
  `main`. Use its remote-tracking ref (`origin/<base>`) when there is one. Diff from
  `git merge-base origin/<base> HEAD` to the working tree (`git diff <merge-base>`), and list the
  untracked files (`git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all`): they are part of the change — read
  them.
- **`#123`, a bare number or a pull-request URL:** that pull request —
  `gh pr view <number> --json number,title,body,url,baseRefName,headRefName,headRefOid,closingIssuesReferences`
  and `gh pr diff <number>`.
- **A branch** (`git rev-parse --verify --quiet <branch>` finds it): `git diff <base>...<branch>`, with
  the base found as above.
- **A path** (it exists in the work tree): the current branch's change limited to it
  (`git diff <merge-base> -- "<path>"`, and its untracked files); if that is empty, review the files
  under it as they are (`git ls-files -- "<path>"`), and say so in the draft.
- **Anything else, or two readings at once** (a branch and a path with the same name): ask me — one
  question, 2–4 options, the recommended one first.

Pass every path to git quoted, after `--`, with forward slashes (`-- "src/my dir/x.rs"`), so a path
with spaces or backslashes reaches git whole. An empty change: say so and stop.

**Not checked out here** — a pull request, or a branch whose head isn't `HEAD`: its code can be read
but not run. Read files at the head commit with `git show <head>:<path>` (fetch it first when it's
missing: `git fetch --quiet origin pull/<number>/head`, or `git fetch --quiet origin <branch>`). Tell
me that the findings can then be confirmed only by reading, and offer that I check it out
(`gh pr checkout <number>`) so they can be reproduced — I run the checkout, not you.

## 1. Collect the context pack
Run these reads in **one message** (they are pre-approved): `git diff --stat` and the diff itself;
`git log --format='%h %s' <base>..<head>`; and the change's stated purpose — the pull request's title
and body, the issues it closes (`gh issue view`), and any intent file in `{{ dir }}/` or issue draft in
`.claude/intent/drafts/` it names. No purpose stated anywhere: use the commit subjects, and say so. If
`gh` isn't installed (the GitHub CLI, https://cli.github.com), isn't logged in (`gh auth login`, which I
run) or can't reach GitHub, say so and continue without it.

The **context pack** is the target, the base and head commits, the stat, the diff, the log and the
purpose. Also list the **changed symbols**: the functions, types, CLI flags, config keys, environment
variables and file formats the diff adds, removes or changes. With a diff of more than about 3,000
lines, split it into groups of related files and give each area one review run per group.

## 2. Find — one wave, in parallel
Launch all of these in **one message**, so they run at once; none waits for another. Give each the
context pack verbatim: an agent without a shell can't run git, and every agent must review the same
commits.
{% if scope_guard -%}
- **Scope:** `{{ scope_guard }}` gets the purpose and the base and head commits, and checks the change
  against the purpose both ways: what the purpose asks that the change lacks (Missing, Partial), what
  the change adds that the purpose doesn't need (Cut, Defer), and the blast radius. Tell it when the
  target isn't checked out: it reads with `git show <head>:<path>`.
{% endif -%}
- **Review — one `{{ review_agent }}` run per area**{% if not review_agent_owned %} (it can edit files: tell each run the review is
  read-only — no edits, and no command that writes){% endif %}, each given the context pack, its area, and
  the severity scale in the comment block at the top of `{{ issue_template }}`:
  - **Correctness:** logic, error and edge paths, state, concurrency, compatibility of public surfaces.
  - **Security:** injection, path traversal, check bypasses, unsafe defaults, secrets, permissions and
    trust boundaries.
  - **Tests:** changed behavior no test covers, tests that don't assert what they claim, flaky or
    order-dependent tests.
  - **Cross-platform:** Windows paths (spaces, backslashes, drive letters, `\\?\` verbatim paths), line
    endings, shell portability (POSIX sh, Git Bash, PowerShell), case-insensitive file systems.
  Each reports only in its area — "nothing in this area" when nothing applies — and gives every
  candidate: the area; severity and likelihood; `file:line`; when [trigger], [behavior], which means
  [impact]; the evidence; the proposed fix; and `Confidence: NN%`.
- **Context:** {% if prefer_explorer %}`explorer`{% else %}a built-in `Explore` subagent{% endif %} gets the changed symbols and maps their consumers — every
  call site, the tests that cover them, the docs that describe them, each with `file:line`. It reports
  facts, not findings.
{% if not scope_guard -%}
- **Scope, yourself:** while they run, check the change against its purpose both ways — what the
  purpose asks that the change lacks, what the change adds that the purpose doesn't need — and state
  the blast radius: files, public surface, dependencies, what could break, how to roll it back.
{% endif %}
## 3. Merge into candidates
Put the reports together. Merge candidates that describe the same flaw — the same cause in the same
place — and keep every area that found it; number the rest **C1, C2, …**. {% if scope_guard %}Each of
`{{ scope_guard }}`'s Missing or Partial items becomes a candidate in the **scope** area; its Cut and
Defer items and the blast radius go to the draft's Scope section, not the findings, unless a reviewer
found a flaw in that code.{% else %}Each gap your scope check found becomes a candidate in the
**scope** area.{% endif %} Attach to each candidate the consumers found for the symbols it touches.
Drop nothing here: a candidate you doubt still goes to the check.

## 4. Verify — every candidate checked on its own
{% if adversary -%}
Give each candidate to `{{ adversary }}` on its own: one run per candidate of Medium or higher, and one
run for all the Low ones together. Launch them in **one message**, at most 8 at a time (the next 8 as
those return). Give each run the candidate's claim, its `file:line` and its proposed fix, the context
pack, and the consumers attached to it — **not** the reviewer's reasoning, severity rationale or
confidence, so the check stands on its own. Ask it to try to disprove the claim, and to return one
verdict per candidate under its **Challenged claims**:
- **Confirmed (reproduced)** — a test, the built program or a PoC input shows it: the exact command and
  what it printed.
- **Confirmed (by reading)** — the code at `file:line` does what the claim says, and a caller can reach
  the trigger: `Confidence: NN%`, and what would reproduce it.
- **Rejected** — the evidence doesn't hold, nothing reaches the trigger, it isn't in this change's code
  or behavior, or it repeats another candidate: the reason, with `file:line`.
- **Unsettled** — it can neither confirm nor reject it (a platform it can't run, a service it can't
  reach): what would settle it.
For each confirmed one, also ask whether the severity fits the scale (and which it should be, with the
evidence), and whether the proposed fix would work ("holds", or the concern). It changes nothing in the
work tree: a PoC that needs files uses a directory from `mktemp -d`. When the target isn't checked out,
tell it so: it reads with `git show <head>:<path>` and can confirm by reading only. A flaw it reports
that no candidate named has had no second look: give it to one more fresh run, the same way, once; a
flaw that run finds in turn goes to Not confirmed.
{% else -%}
No adversary in this project, so nothing gets reproduced: give each candidate to a fresh
`{{ review_agent }}` run on its own — one per candidate of Medium or higher, one for all the Low ones,
in **one message**, at most 8 at a time — with the claim, its `file:line` and fix, the context pack and
the consumers, but **not** the reviewer's reasoning or confidence. It returns **Confirmed (by reading)**
with `Confidence: NN%`, **Rejected** with the reason, or **Unsettled** with what would settle it. So
every surviving finding is **Inferred**: say in the draft that no agent tried to reproduce them.
{% endif %}
Only confirmed candidates become findings. Number them **R1, R2, …** by severity (Critical first), then
likelihood. Where the check gives evidence for another severity, use that. An Inferred finding's
severity is **(provisional)** — the house style lets only Verified findings set one — and comes with
**To verify:** what would confirm it. Rejected and unsettled candidates never become findings and are
never dropped: they go in the draft's last block.

