## 5. Draft the issue, check the tone, show it
If no candidate survived, don't write a draft: show me the rejected and unsettled candidates with
their reasons, and ask whether I want a draft anyway.
- **Title:** imperative and actionable (what to do, not the topic), at most 72 characters — e.g.
  "Fix the two High findings in the retry change before it merges".
- **Description:** follow the writing standard and `{{ issue_template }}` — a guide, not a form: keep
  its sections in their order and what each is for, adapt the wording to a review, and leave its
  `<!-- -->` comments out (if it is missing, use: Intent with the **Blocks prod?** and **Depends on the
  platform view** lines, Options and trade-offs including "do nothing", Proposed call, Needs from the
  approvers, and the Plan in a collapsed `<details>` block).
  - **Intent:** what was reviewed — the target, `<base>..<head>` as short commits, and "plus
    uncommitted changes" when there were any — what should be true before it merges, and one line for
    each finding of Medium or higher: its ID, severity, trigger and impact.
  - **Blocks prod?** Yes when a finding is Critical or High. **Depends on the platform view**: as the
    template asks.
  - **Options and trade-offs:** fix every finding before merging; fix the Critical and High ones now
    and file the rest; do nothing (merge as it is) — each with what it costs and what can go wrong.
  - **Proposed call**, then **Needs from the approvers**: {% if approvers %}name them in that heading ({{ approvers }}),
    then one pending checklist line per approver, as @mentions — GitHub notifies them when I file
    the issue{% else %}"No approvers configured"{% endif %}.
  - Then three collapsed blocks, each `<details><summary>…</summary>`, a blank line, the content and
    `</details>`:
    1. **Findings (R1–Rn)**, one line each, like this:
       ```
       - **R3 · High · Possible · Verified** (cross-platform) — When `--target` is a Windows path with spaces, `resolve_target` (`src/review.rs:88`) splits it, so the review silently skips the change, which means the report is clean for code nobody checked. Evidence: test `target_with_spaces` fails. Fix: pass the path through `paths::for_shell`. {% if adversary %}{{ adversary }}: confirmed (reproduced: `cargo test target_with_spaces`){% else %}{{ review_agent }}: confirmed (by reading){% endif %}.
       ```
       An Inferred one reads `**R5 · Medium (provisional) · Inferred, Confidence 70%**` and ends with
       `To verify: …`. After the findings: **What works** — what the review found correct, in a line or
       two; **Scope** — {% if scope_guard %}`{{ scope_guard }}`'s Missing, Partial, Cut and Defer items that aren't findings,
       and its{% else %}what your scope check found that isn't a finding, and the{% endif %} blast radius; and which agents ran.
    2. **Rejected and not confirmed** — each rejected candidate with its reason
       (`- C7 (security) "<the claim>" — rejected: <reason> (file:line)`), then each **Not confirmed**
       one with what would settle it. Not confirmed is not a finding — no ID, no severity, not in the
       Intent — but it is listed, never dropped.
    3. **Plan** — the template's outline, left as it is until the intent is agreed.
  - **Words:** the visible text — everything outside the `<details>` blocks — is at most {{ max_words }} words;
    the blocks don't count. State both counts.
- **One line per paragraph:** GitHub shows every newline in an issue as a line break, so write each
  paragraph, each list item and each finding on one line — never wrap it by hand. ocgen flags lines
  wrapped mid-sentence after every write to the draft.
- **Name:** `.claude/intent/drafts/issue-review-<slug>.md` — `/intent`'s name for a draft with no
  intent file, so `/intent`, `/deliver` and `/recap` find it. `<slug>` is the target in lowercase
  letters, digits and hyphens — the branch (`feat/retry_budget` → `feat-retry-budget`), `pr-123`, or
  the path — at most 80 characters with its prefix. If that file exists, it holds an earlier review:
  add `-2`, `-3`, … rather than overwrite it. Make sure `.claude/intent/drafts/.gitignore` exists and
  holds `*` (drafts stay out of git).
{% include "intent/tone-check.md" %}
  Then write the draft with the Write tool, and show me its path, the title
  and the description in separate fenced blocks, both word counts, one line on what the check
  changed, and:
  {% include "intent/browser-review.md" %}
- **Never post on the pull request** — no `gh pr comment`, `gh pr review` or API call: the findings
  reach it only through the issue I file, or what I post myself.
- **What comes next is `/intent`'s:** once I file the issue, `/intent #<number>` links it, and
  `/intent issue-review-<slug>` records the sign-offs and fills in the Plan. Say so in one line, then
  offer the ADR (step 6) in one line — and don't write it unless I ask.

## 6. An ADR, only when I ask
Write an ADR only when I ask for one, and only once the draft exists. Re-read the draft first — I may
have changed it in the browser — and build on my version. The ADR is an intent file, numbered and
structured the way `/intent` does it:
{% include "intent/numbering.md" %}
4. **Link them both ways, and move the draft.** `/intent`, `/deliver` and `/recap` look for the draft
   of an intent file under that file's name, lowercased: move the draft there with
   `mv .claude/intent/drafts/<old name>.md .claude/intent/drafts/<the ADR's name, lowercased>.md` (it
   asks me first — that prompt is my confirmation; the draft is git-ignored). Then, with the Edit
   tool, link the ADR from the draft's Intent section ("Findings and decision: `<the ADR's path>`").
   In the ADR, write `Issue: not filed yet — draft .claude/intent/drafts/<the ADR's name, lowercased>.md`
   until I file the issue; `/intent` replaces it with the link.
5. **Check, write, show.** Run the tone check (step 5) on the ADR and the changed draft and fix what it
   reports; then write the ADR{% if reading_copies %} and its reading copy{% endif %}. Show me the ADR's path and the draft's
   new path, and tell me: if the old draft is still open in a browser tab, that tab is stale — close it
   and type `draft` to open the moved one. From here the ADR is an intent like any other:
   `/intent {{ prefix }}-<number>` resumes it for the sign-off, the issue link and the Plan.
